WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:15.077
Mr. Fogg paid the cabman and was about to enter the train station when a poor beggar approached

2
00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:21.323
him. She was carrying a child in her arms, her bare feet were smeared with mud, and her

3
00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:22.704
clothes were old and torn.

4
00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:25.106
She asked Mr.

5
00:00:25.146 --> 00:00:26.548
Fogg for some money for food.

6
00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:32.374
Mr. Fogg took out the twenty guineas he had won that very evening at whist and handed them

7
00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:34.535
to her, saying, "Here, my good woman.

8
00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:36.155
I'm glad that I met you."

9
00:00:37.356 --> 00:00:38.716
Then he continued on his way.

10
00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:44.859
Passepartout was touched by his master's generosity, and he entered the station with a tear in his eye.

11
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.841
Mr. Fogg quickly bought two first-class tickets for Paris.

12
00:00:50.922 --> 00:00:54.023
As he and Passepatout were crossing the platform, Mr.

13
00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:56.504
Fogg saw his five friends from the Reform Club.

14
00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:58.837
"Well, gentlemen," he said.

15
00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:02.700
"I'm off, and you can examine my passport when I get back.

16
00:01:04.042 --> 00:01:06.704
You will be able to judge then whether I completed the journey."

17
00:01:06.724 --> 00:01:10.307
"Oh, that is quite unnecessary, Mr.

18
00:01:10.327 --> 00:01:12.268
Fogg," said one man politely.

19
00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:16.111
"We trust your word as a gentleman of honor."

20
00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:19.594
"You do remember when you are due back in London?"

21
00:01:20.034 --> 00:01:20.995
asked another man.

22
00:01:21.015 --> 00:01:29.453
"In eighty days, on Saturday, December 21, 1872, at 8:45 p.m.," said Mr.

23
00:01:29.473 --> 00:01:31.354
Fogg. "Good-bye, gentlemen."

24
00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:38.637
And with that, Phileas Fogg and Passepartout boarded the train and seated themselves in the first-class carriage.

25
00:01:40.578 --> 00:01:43.900
Five-minutes later the train whistled, and then glided out of the station.

26
00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:50.543
Phileas Fogg suspected that his departure from London would create a lively sensation.

27
00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:52.744
He was right.

28
00:01:54.124 --> 00:01:59.729
The news of the extraordinary bet spread through the Reform Club and sparked excited conversation among its members.

29
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.934
The news quickly got into the newspapers around London, and the "tour of the world" was soon being

30
00:02:05.994 --> 00:02:07.255
talked about all over town.

31
00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:09.596
Some people supported Mr.

32
00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:11.658
Fogg and believed his journey would be a success.

33
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.103
But many thought he was insane for betting so much money on a journey he could not possibly

34
00:02:18.163 --> 00:02:19.364
complete in eighty days.

35
00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:22.307
Many of the newspapers claimed Mr.

36
00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:23.768
Fogg's project was madness.

37
00:02:25.476 --> 00:02:27.918
Only the Daily Telegraph supported his adventure.

38
00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:34.122
Soon the members of the Reform Club weren't the only ones to place wagers on Phileas Fogg's journey.

39
00:02:35.523 --> 00:02:39.026
His name began appearing in the betting books as if he were a racehorse!

40
00:02:40.347 --> 00:02:45.530
Five days after his departure, an article appeared in the bulletin of the Royal Geographical Society.

41
00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:49.473
Everything, it said, was against the travelers.

42
00:02:50.914 --> 00:02:53.676
There would be obstacles imposed by humans and by nature.

43
00:02:55.232 --> 00:03:01.015
Furthermore, Fogg's success would depend on a miraculous agreement of trains and steamers' departure and arrival times.

44
00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:07.437
Mr. Fogg might be able to depend on trains arriving on time in Europe, where distances were relatively

45
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:13.680
short. But when he was trying to cross India in three days, and the United States in seven,

46
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.021
transportation would not be as reliable.

47
00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:22.604
Surely there would be problems with machinery, trains running off the tracks, collisions, and bad weather.

48
00:03:24.732 --> 00:03:29.715
Still, as Phileas Fogg rode the train out of London, he was relaxed and confident in his first-class

49
00:03:29.755 --> 00:03:33.418
carriage, unconcerned about any of the problems that could interrupt his journey.

50
00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:39.702
Passepartout, however, had still not recovered from the shock of being whisked away on such short notice.

51
00:03:41.263 --> 00:03:46.467
He clung mechanically to the carpetbag filled with money and stared straight ahead without seeing a thing.

52
00:03:48.188 --> 00:03:54.472
As the train was speeding through Sydenham, just outside London, Passepartout suddenly uttered a cry of despair.

53
00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:56.857
"What's the matter?"

54
00:03:57.257 --> 00:03:57.798
asked Mr.

55
00:03:57.838 --> 00:04:00.220
Fogg. "Alas!"

56
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:01.641
cried Passepartout.

57
00:04:01.661 --> 00:04:05.384
"In my hurry I forgot "What?"

58
00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:06.445
asked Mr.

59
00:04:06.485 --> 00:04:08.927
Fogg. "To turn off the gas in my room!"

60
00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:10.289
said Passepartout.

61
00:04:10.309 --> 00:04:13.572
"Very well, young man," returned Mr.

62
00:04:13.612 --> 00:04:14.392
Fogg coolly.

63
00:04:14.412 --> 00:04:17.035
"It will burn at your expense."

64
00:04:18.396 --> 00:04:20.398
Passepartout looked down in embarrassment.

65
00:04:21.118 --> 00:04:22.780
It was his fault, after all.

66
00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.922
After arriving in Calais, they traveled by train to Paris and then through the Alps to Turin in

67
00:04:28.982 --> 00:04:29.682
northern Italy.

68
00:04:30.443 --> 00:04:36.865
They traversed Italy to Brindisi, at the heel of Italy's boot, where they boarded the ship Mongolia and

69
00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:37.966
sailed for Suez.

70
00:04:43.088 --> 00:04:43.588
So Mr.

71
00:04:43.648 --> 00:04:48.190
Fogg and I had just caught the train from Paris, France, to Brindisi, a port in the south

72
00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:48.630
of Italy.

73
00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.911
What a long trip it was!

74
00:04:51.751 --> 00:04:53.532
Although I must say, it was extremely comfortable.

75
00:04:54.556 --> 00:04:57.718
After all, this is known as the "golden age" of rail travel.

76
00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:02.101
Train travel is now more popular and comfortable than ever before.

77
00:05:03.322 --> 00:05:08.346
Train tracks first appeared in Europe in the 1500s, but the "train" was a wagon pulled along the

78
00:05:08.406 --> 00:05:09.266
track by horses.

79
00:05:10.307 --> 00:05:14.910
In 1804 an English steam engine pulled train cars with passengers for the first time.

80
00:05:16.832 --> 00:05:19.854
Early steam engines like this were often called "iron horses."

81
00:05:20.574 --> 00:05:22.856
I'm just glad I didn't have to "feed" it the coal it needed!

82
00:05:23.776 --> 00:05:24.917
What a tough and dirty job!

83
00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:28.098
The first passenger trains were pretty rough.

84
00:05:29.459 --> 00:05:35.423
They seldom traveled more than 18 miles per hour, passengers sat on wooden benches, and meals were eaten

85
00:05:35.503 --> 00:05:37.424
quickly in dining halls at train stations.

86
00:05:38.784 --> 00:05:41.306
Thankfully, train travel has improved a lot since then.

87
00:05:42.286 --> 00:05:47.629
By the 1860s trains could travel at around 99 miles per hour, and no more wooden benches!

88
00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.312
Passengers now have a choice of first, second, or third class.

89
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.024
First class is like a hotel on wheels!

90
00:05:56.226 --> 00:06:00.173
The rooms are large and luxurious, and the dining car resembles a fancy restaurant.

91
00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:06.204
There are also bars and lounges where passengers can relax, and watch the countryside go by.
